{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>Adding one badge to an avatar meant editing four files that don't render an avatar</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A dashboard fetches the current user once, at the top, in <code>&lt;Dashboard&gt;</code>. The component that actually needs it, <code>&lt;Avatar&gt;</code>, sits five levels down: <code>Dashboard &rarr; Layout &rarr; Sidebar &rarr; UserPanel &rarr; Avatar</code>. So <code>user</code> gets passed as a prop into <code>Layout</code>, which passes it into <code>Sidebar</code>, which passes it into <code>UserPanel</code>, which finally hands it to <code>Avatar</code>. None of those three middle components render anything about the user, they just take the prop in and immediately pass it back out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then a new feature needs a <code>notificationCount</code> badge on the same avatar. That value also has to travel through <code>Layout</code>, <code>Sidebar</code>, and <code>UserPanel</code>, none of which care about it either. Three files get edited for a feature that visually only touches one component.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is prop drilling: every component between the state's owner and its consumer becomes coupled to a prop it never reads, just forwards. It's not a correctness bug, the app still works, it's a maintenance tax that compounds. Each new piece of deep data means editing every intermediate component's signature and every call site that renders it, even though those components' actual behavior never changes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The deeper cost isn't the typing, it's the coupling it creates. <code>Sidebar</code> and <code>UserPanel</code> now have <code>user</code> and <code>notificationCount</code> in their prop types even though neither component has any logic that depends on either value. Anyone reading <code>Sidebar</code>'s signature has to trace two more components down before learning those props are just being relayed, and refactoring <code>Sidebar</code> (splitting it, reordering its children) risks breaking a data path it doesn't actually participate in.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Pass the already-rendered component down, not the raw data through every layer</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of giving <code>Layout</code> and <code>Sidebar</code> a <code>user</code> prop to forward, the component that owns the data renders the deep child itself, <code>&lt;Avatar user={user} /&gt;</code>, and passes that already-built element down as <code>children</code> (or a named slot prop). <code>Layout</code> and <code>Sidebar</code> just render <code>{children}</code> wherever it belongs in their own markup. They never see <code>user</code> at all, because they're not the ones creating the element that needs it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Concretely: <code>Dashboard</code> builds the tree top-down, <code>&lt;Layout sidebar={&lt;Sidebar userPanel={&lt;UserPanel avatar={&lt;Avatar user={user} /&gt;} /&gt;} /&gt;} /&gt;</code>, or more idiomatically nests it as JSX children rather than named slot props. Either way, the data flows directly from where it's owned to where it's consumed; the intermediate components only handle layout, arranging whatever element was handed to them. Their prop signatures describe what they actually do (render a sidebar shape) instead of what they merely relay.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is React's composition model doing the job people often reach for prop drilling (or prematurely, Context) to solve. It specifically fits data with one owner and one consumer connected by a chain of purely structural components, exactly this case's shape. It's the wrong tool when the same piece of data is genuinely needed at many scattered, unrelated points in the tree, that's the shape Context is for, not this one, and reaching for composition there just relocates the coupling instead of removing it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>Every new sidebar feature meant a four-file diff that touched no sidebar logic</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's dashboard passed <code>currentUser</code>, <code>theme</code>, and <code>permissions</code> down through <code>Layout &rarr; Sidebar &rarr; NavPanel &rarr; UserBadge</code>. Every one of those three middle components existed to arrange layout, not to use any of those three props. When product asked for a small unread-notifications badge on the same avatar, the team added a fourth prop, <code>notificationCount</code>, and once again touched <code>Layout</code>, <code>Sidebar</code>, and <code>NavPanel</code> just to get it past them.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Code review kept flagging the same pattern: PRs for small, cosmetic Avatar-area features consistently touched three or four unrelated files, and reviewers had to check each one just to confirm it was purely a pass-through and not something that also, coincidentally, used the value.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The refactor moved <code>Layout</code>, <code>Sidebar</code>, and <code>NavPanel</code> to accept <code>children</code> instead of typed data props, and had <code>Dashboard</code> compose the whole tree itself: build <code>&lt;UserBadge user={currentUser} notificationCount={n} /&gt;</code>, hand it to <code>NavPanel</code> as children, hand that to <code>Sidebar</code>, hand that to <code>Layout</code>. After the refactor, the next deep feature (an avatar status dot) touched exactly two files, <code>Dashboard</code> and <code>UserBadge</code>, and code review time on Avatar-area PRs dropped correspondingly, because there was nothing left in the middle to review.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

