{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Composition isn't the fix for every deep-data problem, and neither is prop drilling always wrong</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Passing a prop through one, or even two, intermediate components isn't a real problem, it's explicit and easy to trace. The pain shows up around three or more untouched layers, or when the same data needs to reach several different, unrelated branches of the tree. Don't reach for a bigger pattern to solve a two-level pass-through; that's adding complexity, not removing it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Composition specifically fits data that has one owner and flows to one consumer through purely structural components. When the same value (an authenticated user, a locale, a theme) is genuinely needed at many scattered, unrelated points across the tree, not just one path, Context (or a state library) is the right tool instead; composing that data through every leaf that happens to need it just relocates the coupling rather than removing it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Context has its own cost worth knowing before reaching for it reflexively: every consumer of a context re-renders when its value changes, so a context value that changes often and is read by many components can cause broad re-render fan-out unless the value is split into narrower providers or memoized carefully. Composition doesn't have this failure mode, the data isn't flowing through a shared subscription at all, it's just JSX passed as a prop, so intermediate components that don't consume it don't re-render because of it. That's a real advantage composition has over Context for the specific "one path, one destination" shape, worth stating explicitly rather than treating Context as the universal upgrade from prop drilling.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

