{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">If a component only takes a prop to immediately pass it to a child, that's prop drilling — pass the already-built child element down instead, so the middle components never need that prop at all.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Compose the tree from the top: the owner of the data renders the deep consumer directly and hands it down as children, so intermediate layout components only ever describe layout.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Composition fits one-owner, one-consumer data flowing through structural components. Data needed at many scattered points across the tree is a Context problem, not a composition one — don't use either where a simple one- or two-level prop is fine as-is.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
